♫ Expresso keep me goin'

00:04:55.426 --> 00:04:58.588
- Hey Believe Nation we're
working on a special top 10

00:04:58.588 --> 00:05:01.405
of Neil Strauss as well
and here's a quick preview

00:05:01.405 --> 00:05:03.209
of some of the clips
we're going to include.

00:05:03.209 --> 00:05:04.068
I hope you enjoy.

00:05:04.068 --> 00:05:07.964
- Number one rule of writing is this.

00:05:07.964 --> 00:05:09.525
Nobody cares.

00:05:09.525 --> 00:05:12.521
Nobody cares about you, they
don't care about your thoughts,

00:05:12.521 --> 00:05:14.297
they don't care about your ideas,

00:05:14.297 --> 00:05:15.330
they don't care about
what you have to say,

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:20.276
what you did yesterday, or
anything, the do not give a shit.

00:05:20.276 --> 00:05:23.123
And from there, can you make them care?

00:05:23.123 --> 00:05:26.490
Everything I write, I
assume that nobody cares.

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:27.813
That they have no interest.

00:05:27.813 --> 00:05:28.882
And if I start from that perspective,

00:05:28.882 --> 00:05:30.774
then if I can draw you in,

00:05:30.774 --> 00:05:33.456
through the writing, that's
where the story begins.

00:05:33.456 --> 00:05:34.617
So just assume.

00:05:34.617 --> 00:05:35.929
Start by assuming nobody cares

00:05:35.929 --> 00:05:40.898
and how can you draw them
into your story or your point.

00:05:40.898 --> 00:05:42.454
Number two, then.

00:05:42.454 --> 00:05:45.252
One way to draw them in is
something called Open Loops.

00:05:45.252 --> 00:05:48.630
Who here knows what an Open Loop is?

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.929
Has anyone seen the new Dr. Strange movie?

00:05:50.929 --> 00:05:52.194
Any one seen that?

00:05:52.194 --> 00:05:53.533
Cool, Cal you saw that, so

00:05:53.533 --> 00:05:54.366
is it good?

00:05:54.366 --> 00:05:55.770
Okay, by the way that's an Open Loop.

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:57.953
An Open Loop was I asked
you what an Open Loop was,

00:05:57.953 --> 00:05:59.311
and then rather than explaining it,

00:05:59.311 --> 00:06:00.345
I went on to another subject

00:06:00.345 --> 00:06:02.284
and talked about Dr. Strange or something.

00:06:02.284 --> 00:06:04.629
So an Open Loop is, if I
can start with something

00:06:04.629 --> 00:06:06.161
that captures your attention,

00:06:06.161 --> 00:06:08.460
and 98% of you didn't
know what an Open Loop is,

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:09.644
you're probably curious about it now

00:06:09.644 --> 00:06:10.759
because I've been talking for two minutes

00:06:10.759 --> 00:06:12.036
and still haven't explained it yet.

00:06:12.036 --> 00:06:14.277
Right, an Open Loop is at the top,

00:06:14.277 --> 00:06:16.622
can I tell you something
that you're interested in

00:06:16.622 --> 00:06:18.642
and you want to know the answer
to, and wait 'til the end.

00:06:18.642 --> 00:06:21.661
How many times have we stayed
through a really shitty movie

00:06:21.661 --> 00:06:23.472
just so you want to know what
happens at the end, right?

00:06:23.472 --> 00:06:25.608
It can be the worst movie ever,

00:06:25.608 --> 00:06:27.326
but I want to know how that happened.

00:06:27.326 --> 00:06:30.354
Right, so one is to use
the idea of Open Loops,

00:06:30.354 --> 00:06:32.130
and it's true in relationships,

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:33.408
you meet somebody you
have a lot in common with

00:06:33.408 --> 00:06:35.103
and start telling all these stories,

00:06:35.103 --> 00:06:36.705
you want it to continue.

00:06:36.705 --> 00:06:38.853
So an Open Loop is a great
way to create intrigue

00:06:38.853 --> 00:06:41.569
'cuz we're creatures
who love story telling.

00:06:41.569 --> 00:06:46.202
Right, early, early, story
telling is part of our DNA.

00:06:46.202 --> 00:06:50.358
So creating Open Loops at the
top of a post, or in a book,

00:06:50.358 --> 00:06:51.821
is really powerful.

00:06:51.821 --> 00:06:54.584
Four things, when someone gives
you feedback or criticism,

00:06:54.584 --> 00:06:56.302
however it's intended,
whether ill or positive,

00:06:56.302 --> 00:06:59.646
and so one is not to take it
in, but to kind of catch it

00:06:59.646 --> 00:07:00.795
I think of it like a catchers' mitt,

00:07:00.795 --> 00:07:03.164
if you tell me something,
you come across this way,

00:07:03.164 --> 00:07:05.637
I'll grab it and then I'll
look at it and I'll think

00:07:05.637 --> 00:07:06.666
"Okay, is it true?"

00:07:06.666 --> 00:07:07.918
If it's true, cool,

00:07:07.918 --> 00:07:09.213
I'll put it in and then
I'll learn from it.

00:07:09.213 --> 00:07:11.233
If I think it's not true,
cool, I'll throw it away,

00:07:11.233 --> 00:07:12.185
no harm, no foul.

00:07:12.185 --> 00:07:13.137
I'm not going to dwell on it.

00:07:13.137 --> 00:07:13.970
- Yeah.
- Right.

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:15.278
And I think everybody
needs this in this world

00:07:15.278 --> 00:07:17.653
'cuz we're all on, most
of us are on social media,

00:07:17.653 --> 00:07:18.686
and we're all getting like--

00:07:18.686 --> 00:07:19.519
- (Laughs)

00:07:19.519 --> 00:07:20.947
- All our thoughts are
getting picked apart.

00:07:20.947 --> 00:07:21.952
- Right.
- All the time.

00:07:21.952 --> 00:07:23.638
So we really need this more than ever.

00:07:23.638 --> 00:07:26.357
Then the third one is, you
get it and it's a maybe,

00:07:26.357 --> 00:07:27.190
well I'm not sure.

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:28.491
You said this about me, I'm not sure.

00:07:28.491 --> 00:07:29.662
What do you guys think?

00:07:29.662 --> 00:07:30.495
What do you think?

00:07:30.495 --> 00:07:32.137
I'll ask my friends and
I'll ask other people

00:07:32.137 --> 00:07:34.656
and get some feedback and
if it's true, well cool,

00:07:34.656 --> 00:07:35.562
maybe I'll learn from it.

00:07:35.562 --> 00:07:36.792
If everyone's like this
"It's total nonsense,

00:07:36.792 --> 00:07:38.151
"ignore that dude."

00:07:38.151 --> 00:07:39.346
I throw it out.

00:07:39.346 --> 00:07:40.496
But then there's a fourth one,

00:07:40.496 --> 00:07:42.156
and this is where the real growth is.

00:07:42.156 --> 00:07:42.989
Do you know what the fourth one is?

00:07:42.989 --> 00:07:43.822
- I don't.

00:07:43.822 --> 00:07:47.624
- The fourth one is, you
get it, you throw it out.

00:07:47.624 --> 00:07:48.457
I'm talkin' to you

00:07:48.457 --> 00:07:50.829
and then maybe later I'm talkin' to you,

00:07:50.829 --> 00:07:53.023
I get it, and I'm like "Nah",
so should I throw it out,

00:07:53.023 --> 00:07:55.252
and then later I'm talkin'
to someone else, I get it.

00:07:55.252 --> 00:07:56.286
It's the third time it's come back to me,

00:07:56.286 --> 00:07:57.119
I've thrown it out.

00:07:57.119 --> 00:07:57.952
I'm like "Wait, maybe I've got to

00:07:57.952 --> 00:07:58.960
"take this thing a little more seriously"

00:07:58.960 --> 00:07:59.793
- [Host] Right.

00:07:59.793 --> 00:08:01.381
- And it becomes a maybe,
and then it becomes a yes.

00:08:01.381 --> 00:08:02.891
And that's when the big growth thing is,

00:08:02.891 --> 00:08:05.364
because you kept ignoring this
thing that was in your face.

00:08:05.364 --> 00:08:07.790
So the best feedback, like
where you really grow,

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:09.555
is you keep getting the same feedback,

00:08:09.555 --> 00:08:11.598
you keep dismissing it, at
some point you say "Wait,

00:08:11.598 --> 00:08:13.200
"maybe they have something."

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:17.600
So one way I do this is, I get
together a group of people,

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:20.576
and we sit together once a
week and talk about stuff

00:08:20.576 --> 00:08:22.259
going on in our lives.

00:08:22.259 --> 00:08:24.683
I find group therapy really powerful,

00:08:24.683 --> 00:08:26.900
because if it's just one on one,

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:28.131
I can just say I disagree with you,

00:08:28.131 --> 00:08:29.826
but when four of five
people are saying "Hey dude,

00:08:29.826 --> 00:08:31.184
"no you're really coming off like that,

00:08:31.184 --> 00:08:33.599
"or you're really having this
problem in your relationship

00:08:33.599 --> 00:08:35.018
"and maybe need to look at it."

00:08:35.018 --> 00:08:36.458
And five people are saying it.

00:08:36.458 --> 00:08:38.768
It's pretty hard to dismiss them.

00:08:38.768 --> 00:08:40.986
We treat ourselves horribly.

00:08:40.986 --> 00:08:44.736
We talk to ourselves in ways
that we would never ever

00:08:44.736 --> 00:08:47.731
allow a stranger to talk to us, right?

00:08:47.731 --> 00:08:51.198
If somebody here walked up to you and said

00:08:51.198 --> 00:08:53.275
"You're not interesting,
you've got nothing good to say,

00:08:53.275 --> 00:08:55.784
"you're not enough and
you're not attractive."

00:08:55.784 --> 00:08:58.476
You'd be like "Fuck you, let's fight."

00:08:58.476 --> 00:09:01.226
(crowd laughing)

00:09:02.329 --> 00:09:06.496
So, why do we allow ourselves
to talk to ourselves that way?

00:09:08.072 --> 00:09:10.127
So if there's sort of a message
I can give you guys today,

00:09:10.127 --> 00:09:12.867
that's going to turn and help
you get over approaching anxiety,

00:09:12.867 --> 00:09:15.630
is start treating yourself
the way you want that person

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:18.151
you're approaching to treat you, right?

00:09:18.151 --> 00:09:21.564
If she said that she's not going
to say anything half as mean,

00:09:21.564 --> 00:09:23.898
as the shit you're telling yourself,

00:09:23.898 --> 00:09:27.071
you know, so you got nothing
to fear anymore, right?

00:09:27.071 --> 00:09:29.173
'Cuz you've already given
yourself the worst of it.

00:09:29.173 --> 00:09:32.191
If I could give you one
thing this whole weekend,

00:09:32.191 --> 00:09:34.653
it'd be to stop talking
to yourself this way.

00:09:34.653 --> 00:09:37.656
So every emotion has it's
corresponding action.

00:09:37.656 --> 00:09:40.117
You may feel a fear of rejection, right?

00:09:40.117 --> 00:09:42.288
But where is that coming from,
like you were saying earlier,

00:09:42.288 --> 00:09:45.353
if you're giving someone worth and value,

00:09:45.353 --> 00:09:47.826
higher status than yourself
and you're less than them,

00:09:47.826 --> 00:09:50.055
what's that saying?

00:09:50.055 --> 00:09:51.286
That's shame.

00:09:51.286 --> 00:09:52.424
Shame about who you are.

00:09:52.424 --> 00:09:54.699
Shame that you're not worthy
to talk to this person.

00:09:54.699 --> 00:09:55.802
Shame that this person

00:09:55.802 --> 00:09:57.497
somehow is going to'
have some power over you,

00:09:57.497 --> 00:09:59.564
or going to hurt you,
that you're not enough,

00:09:59.564 --> 00:10:01.329
that you're not good enough,
you're not attractive enough,

00:10:01.329 --> 00:10:02.815
that you're not rich enough,

00:10:02.815 --> 00:10:04.173
you're not socially comfortable enough,

00:10:04.173 --> 00:10:05.032
that you're too awkward.

00:10:05.032 --> 00:10:07.134
What ever it is, that's
shame about who you are.

00:10:07.134 --> 00:10:08.329
And what happens, right,

00:10:08.329 --> 00:10:12.079
you're shame when you
walked in turns to joy.

00:10:13.368 --> 00:10:14.390
So the overall idea is

00:10:14.390 --> 00:10:16.538
if you can approach from a place of joy,

00:10:16.538 --> 00:10:19.951
we offer a place of joy
instead of a place of shame,

00:10:19.951 --> 00:10:22.633
you will have no approach anxiety.

00:10:22.633 --> 00:10:25.501
That's what we call, back
in the dang ging days,

00:10:25.501 --> 00:10:26.751
being In State.

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:29.541
You know, being in state,

00:10:29.541 --> 00:10:31.666
is just what, really, having joy is.

00:10:31.666 --> 00:10:33.471
The overall thing is if you
walk out and you're like

00:10:33.471 --> 00:10:35.497
"Okay, alright, there all these people

00:10:35.497 --> 00:10:36.611
"and they're going to approve of me,

00:10:36.611 --> 00:10:38.423
"and if they approve of me I'm
going to feel good about myself,

00:10:38.423 --> 00:10:40.152
"but if they don't approve
of me I'm going to feel shit,

00:10:40.152 --> 00:10:42.579
"myself, and I'm going to go
out and improve my self-esteem

00:10:42.579 --> 00:10:44.843
"in the hands of every
stranger I talk to."

00:10:44.843 --> 00:10:46.677
You're just setting
yourself up for trouble.

00:10:46.677 --> 00:10:48.082
You know, you can push past it.

00:10:48.082 --> 00:10:50.009
Using a lot of the stuff I talk about.

00:10:50.009 --> 00:10:52.964
But you're starting off with
a giant, giant handicap.

